\f1\b\fs36 FUZZ\
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::2><!$Scr_Ps::1>
\f2\fs36 Control how floating point numbers are compared.  Takes 1 or 2 parameters\

\f3 \
FUZZ 12, +-1.0E-64
\f2   (defaults)\
\
The first one is the number of digits (ignoring decimal point) to use when comparing two non-zero numbers together. Essentially controlling the epsilon of the comparisons\
\
The second argument is the magnitude of the number to consider a zero.  In floating-point land, there's a discontinuity between the smallest represented positive / negative integer (+-8.9... E-304) and actual zero, so this bridges that gap\
\
SET DEGREE / RADIAN / GRAD\
Controls the representation of the angles that trigonometric functions work with.  Should be obvious\
\
ALPHASCALE/ALPHAROTATE, etc\
These are commands sent to peripheral devices on the GPIB (general purpose interface bus) to control things like how much to scale alphanumerics (text) and rotating them. 
\f4\i We don't have that connected to our 405Xs, so generally ignoring
\f2\i0 \
\
CHARSIZE\
4054 only.  There's 5 or 6 different font sizes, which can give you a massive screen density of text.  
\f4\i The 4054 is not working, so don't care
\f2\i0 \
\
FONT\
Choose different display fonts.  Some are language fonts (such as for English or Dutch), and some are "graphics" and "business" fonts.  
\f4\i Need to play with these to see what's actually in the fonts.
